AIxP Dashboard SME Onboarding Guide
Updated 27 October 2026
Start here
Maintain an executive view of AI consumption and directional spend across Anthropic, OpenAI, GitHub Copilot and Copilot Cowork. The report supports the COO forum and related stakeholders; it is not an invoice, budget-reconciliation or Finance expense system. Preserve that distinction in measures, labels and consumer communications.
The inspected AI Platform Economics Dashboard-new.pbix is a thin report, live connected to the AI Platform Economics Dashboard semantic model in the AI Platform Economics workspace. It contains no embedded model or Power Query mashup. Use the upstream semantic-model project to inspect or change M, DAX, relationships and RLS; the report file alone cannot establish those definitions.
The report contains five pages: Read Me, Overview, Organization, Models and Users. The confirmed workspace URL is below; semantic-model role definitions, role membership and deployed security remain unverified. 
Set up your development baseline
Open the AI Platform Economics workspace: https://app.powerbi.com/groups/5e413bf1-69f1-4c08-9050-e320acd1e78c/list?ctid=41b8c056-2dc3-438d-99ee-61e00287adfe&experience=power-bi
Before editing, obtain the maintained upstream semantic-model project and record its version, report connection, app link, refresh schedule, credentials configuration and any gateway or cloud connection. Confirm access to the source site and user extracts. Keep a restorable project version and confirm the target workspace before publishing.
Create a development copy and capture a baseline with identical date, platform and spend-basis selections: rows, consumption spend, credits and tokens separately, distinct active users, unmatched identities and latest available date by platform. Review query dependencies before changing shared helpers. Make targeted changes and preserve Region, User ID and SBL through every transformation.
Start by reproducing a refresh in development, reconciling one closed day per platform to its source and tracing one user from source to visual. Then test the app as a restricted consumer and demonstrate rollback. 
Understand the model contract
Use this documented data flow as a checklist against the actual upstream project, not as proof of its deployed configuration. Source_SharePoint supplies vendor extracts and user-list files. Vendor queries shape Usage; People supplies user attributes. RegionMap enriches office codes, PersonType supplies associate classification, and PeopleEmailKey translates full email to PersonKey. Anthropic joins spend and usage before identity enrichment. Inspect Usage’s append to identify the active Cowork source.
The common Usage columns are Date, PersonKey, Platform, Model where available, Tokens, Credits, Consumption, ConsumptionUnit and CostUSD. Anthropic also carries requests; GitHub retains discount_amount and net_amount. Keep names and capitalization stable. Date must be a date, PersonKey a normalized text alias, and credits/spend decimal-capable. Remove nested tables and binaries from final outputs. A missing field in one appended source does not by itself remove that field from every other source.
Require People[PersonKey] to be unique and nonblank on the one side of its relationship to Usage[PersonKey]. With ordinary single-direction filtering, People filters Usage, not the reverse. Inspect the current Usage-to-SeatRate platform relationship, active state and cardinality, and confirm how Usage[Date] is used. Do not add a date table or disconnect SeatRate without reviewing the upstream relationships and measures together.
Check ValueBasis, SpendBasis and any TopNBreak helper against the upstream model. Preserve the slicer values used by SELECTEDVALUE branches. Treat a calendar-table change as a separate migration with measure and relationship tests, not as a repair that can be copied from a historical build guide.
Maintain source calculations
Anthropic
Select claude_user_cost_report and claude_user_usage_report files from the API collection, excluding duplicate organizational copies. Join on FileDate + user_email + model + product. Preserve all four keys and test uniqueness at that grain before expanding: multiple matches can multiply spend rows. Map amount_usd to CostUSD and total_tokens to Tokens. Do not substitute amount or list_amount_usd without validating the methodology change.
OpenAI
Use chatgpt_cost_analytics_<date>.csv with type = COSTS. Map billing_estimated_cost_usd_value to CostUSD and billing_cost_value to Credits; never substitute credits for dollars. Include valid dated subfolders rather than restricting the selection to the API root. Inventory selected filenames and reporting dates before changing folder or file exclusions.
Check the token formula’s input, cached-input and output fields against vendor definitions before adding them: cached input may already be included in input. Priced count-based rows can have blank tokens, and token-bearing rows can have no billing value. Neither proves no activity or no invoicing. Validate the grain before deduplicating repeated event IDs or similar rows. Do not add separate user-analytics or credit-usage exports without checking overlap with the configured cost-analytics load.
GitHub Copilot
Map quantity to Credits and gross_amount to CostUSD; retain discount_amount and net_amount separately. Consumption is AI credits, not tokens. Normalize username by removing a trailing _capgroup suffix and lowercasing the alias. Gross spend is not an invoice: discounts, net amounts and contractual treatment need separate reconciliation. Do not extrapolate monthly correctness from one day’s run rate.
Dates and financial meaning
The legacy queries derive reporting dates from filenames. Validate filename patterns against in-file windows and use explicit date/number conversion conventions; do not infer an extract's period from upload time. Spend is directional valuation, not Finance expense. Rate checks can reveal swapped units but cannot detect all duplicated rows. Reconcile both row-level values and file coverage for the exact same reporting period.
Test the Cowork transition
Legacy Cowork exports carry Monthly credits used, a cumulative monthly value. Do not sum successive snapshots. Keeping the latest person/month record avoids repeated snapshots but assigns the monthly total to one date; it is not daily usage. Inventory available history and reconcile monthly totals before replacing this approach.
Check returned date coverage on every refresh. The described export window runs from the previous month’s beginning through the export date, with a typical reporting lag of about two days that can vary. A rolling window drops older periods as time advances. Test persistent history retention before adoption. For overlapping snapshots, select an authoritative version per reporting slice rather than summing repeated exports; later versions can revise prior values.
The test valuation uses credits multiplied by $0.008; reconfirm that rate’s applicability before release. Cowork credits are not raw tokens. If a legacy query copies credits into Tokens for compatibility, assess every affected measure and label before correcting it. Do not combine vendor credits or raw tokens into an unlabeled comparable total.
Switch by explicitly editing Usage's append to include the approved Cowork query exactly once. Renaming a query may update dependent references to the renamed old query; it does not reliably switch the append. Disabling Enable load prevents a separate model table but does not stop a referenced query feeding Usage. Retain a rollback copy and verify the dependency graph, source date coverage and platform totals after the switch.
Protect identity and organizational attribution
Normalize PersonKey before grouping: lowercase, explicitly remove non-breaking spaces, trim, then exclude null or empty aliases. Cleaning after grouping can collapse separate groups into duplicate keys. Recheck uniqueness after RegionMap and PersonType merges; duplicate lookup matches can multiply People rows. Fix the lookup or selection rule instead of deleting arbitrary final rows.
Retain people with blank u_lob_2; do not use a HasLOB filter to exclude them. A missing attribute is not a missing person. Unmatched activity can remain in raw totals under a blank relationship member while named-user or organizational views omit it. Compare raw and attributed totals and keep unmatched usage visible for investigation.
Inspect the People row-selection rule. Prioritizing a row with LOB before the latest FileDate can retain stale SBL, manager, email or other attributes. Rank on a validated extract-as-of date, not Date modified or upload time. To correct historical backfill, test taking the latest base record and filling only approved missing fields from history; quantify the attribution change before release.
Use full lowercase, trimmed email in PeopleEmailKey and in the Anthropic and Cowork merge inputs. Never reuse that name for email local parts. Require one resolved PersonKey per email and flag conflicting mappings; Table.Distinct is not a rule for choosing an arbitrary person. Preserve unmatched usage. OpenAI and GitHub derive aliases directly and need not change merely because the email bridge changes.
Build PersonType from eligible extracts containing u_cg_person_type_code, with normalized aliases and a documented newest-value rule. Map Employee to Associate, other populated categories to Non-Associate, and missing values to Unknown. Neither an absent field nor an old AsOfDate proves that someone departed. Use an authoritative status source for departure or retirement.
Preserve Region, OfficeCode and uppercase User ID through changes. RegionMap must have one row per normalized OfficeCode; retain unmatched offices as a visible unknown category. SBL comes from u_bo_fier_sbl and is the requested display label. Confirm its business definition and chosen extract for disputed assignments instead of inferring it from the manager hierarchy.
Apply LOB-CG mappings consistently
Maintain the mapping from job function and u_lob_2 together with LOB-CG Sort. Legal and Compliance map to GBS - L&C; Global Risk Management to GBS - GRM; Workplace Experience to GBS - Workplace Experience. Map the exact truncated source label GBS Data Analytics Rotations and Trainin to NACG with sort value 2, not EDO or a new GBS line. Existing EDO job-function rows remain EDO.
If a label appears split, test LOB-CG alone with a count, then add matrix row fields one at a time. Check text normalization, selected fields, drill state and hidden filters. A sort value controls ordering; different sort values do not themselves create two identical categories. Require one sort value per label and measure affected active users in the current reporting context.
Maintain measures and the consumer experience
Consumption Spend sums Usage[CostUSD]. Total Spend adds Seat Spend in the documented design, while Spend Measure chooses the licence basis. Total Tokens sums Tokens and Total Consumption sums Consumption; they do not imply equivalent units across vendors. Inspect Display Value and all platform-specific wrappers whenever changing source units. Preserve the technical CostUSD column while using spend in consumer labels.
Validate Licensed Seats against actual provisioned seats; active users are not a substitute. Review calendar-year logic, distinct person/platform/month counts, contracted rates, period eligibility and allocation. Do not repeat monthly seat spend on every daily usage row. Validate rates and invoicing policy with Finance, especially after billing-model changes.
For spend per token, numerator and denominator must cover the same priced, token-bearing rows and exclude licence spend and credit-only platforms. Show count-billed spend without inventing tokens. For share-of-usage, define the intended denominator and remove only the relevant grouping filter while retaining date/platform context. Attribution coverage must test actual membership in People, not just nonblank Usage keys. Active-user counts should consider eligible credit activity as well as tokens and avoid counting a blank key.
Test all five inspected pages: Read Me, Overview, Organization, Models and Users. Check week boundaries, month labels, matrix row layouts, unit labels, both toggle selections, empty states, drill-through and reset controls. Clear-all-slicers does not reset every filter or drill state; maintain page bookmarks against the intended default. Hidden exclusions for blank People[User ID] and Other LOB-CG affect report presentation and attribution, not row-level security.
Validate row-level security as a consumer
Open the upstream semantic-model project and inspect the actual role filters, any dynamic identity logic, entitlement tables and security-filter propagation through relationships. In the service, inspect role membership and the users or groups behind it. Reconcile entitlement keys with the model’s normalized identity keys. Role definitions and membership are not verified by the thin PBIX, so do not invent a role design or describe security as deployed.
Use model role simulation for an initial check, then test the published app and report with a restricted, non-elevated consumer account. Do not use a workspace Admin, Member or Contributor account as proof that RLS works. Check effective permissions from workspace access, app audiences, direct sharing, semantic-model permissions and group membership, including Build and export capabilities.
Run positive and negative checks: confirm an entitled user sees the intended scope, cannot see another scope, and an unentitled user cannot retrieve restricted rows. Include blank or unmatched identity keys and test users with multiple permitted scopes or multiple role memberships. Compare the combined result with the intended access; do not assume a narrower role overrides a broader grant.
Retest after revoking an entitlement or group membership. Allow for the applicable propagation and refresh cycle, start a fresh consumer session, and confirm that removed access no longer works. Repeat permitted export and drill-through tests, including summarized and underlying data where available. Record expected versus actual rows, the account’s effective permissions and the test date; stop release if restricted data is exposed.
Operate, troubleshoot and release
After each refresh, check source freshness separately for each platform, file coverage, query errors, duplicates at the expected grain, People key uniqueness and unmatched usage. Record row counts and consumption spend before and after identity merges. Investigate unexplained changes against exact dates and source files rather than extrapolating one day's values. An overall maximum date can conceal one stale platform.
Symptom	First checks	Safe response
Blank visuals	Usage rows, date type, filters, relationships and append inputs	Find the first failing step; preserve totals and restore only the changed dependency.
Duplicate PersonKey	Cleanup order; duplicate RegionMap or PersonType matches	Normalize before grouping; fix lookup uniqueness; recheck after each merge.
Missing users or SBL	Source row, blank-LOB exclusion, chosen extract date, identity match	Retain users; separate missing attributes from missing people; do not infer employment status.
Missing or duplicate EmailKey	Column creation and both merge inputs	Create once; use PeopleEmailKey consistently; compare actual full-email values.
Unexpected spend	Currency/credit columns, selected files, join row growth, seat basis	Reconcile source rows and measures separately; do not dedupe on weak keys.
Viva refresh blocked	Source-only preview, query status/access and service refresh details	Keep the test isolated; escalate unresolved source availability; use an approved rollback path.
Complete the open validation checks
Before release, verify Cowork credit labels and token measures; extract-date ranking for People and SBL; conflicting email mappings; LOB-CG display and sorting; provisioned-seat basis; source-specific token completeness; and restricted-consumer security tests. Record the evidence, remaining limitation and retest result. Do not treat historical reference documents as proof these checks passed.
Publishing and rollback
Before release, save the previous approved project version and record changes to queries, measures, relationships, mappings and labels. Refresh in development and compare all platforms with the baseline. Obtain business sign-off for hierarchy or spend changes. Test authentication and unattended refresh in the target service; Desktop success alone is insufficient. Check privacy settings for combining organizational sources without weakening controls.
Publish to the confirmed target workspace, verify the report’s connection to the intended semantic model, then update the app intentionally. Test current app, report and model permissions with a restricted consumer account. An app is a distribution experience, not an automatic security boundary. Do not broaden access as a workaround for a failed security test.
If validation fails, stop the release and restore the approved project or connection configuration. Explicitly point Usage at the previous approved Cowork input if needed; do not depend on renaming or Enable load to change the append. Preserve failing-source evidence and history files. Retest freshness and totals after rollback, then restore the approved app version or communicate the temporary data limitation.
Confirm readiness and keep references handy
Complete onboarding when you can trace each platform into Usage, explain spend and consumption units, demonstrate unique People keys and controlled unmatched identities, reconcile a closed reporting day, refresh in the service, validate restricted-consumer access and make a small reversible development change. Keep the baseline, change log, test evidence and rollback instructions with the maintained project.
